        VGA90 is part of the VGAFontMax package.  If you continue to use
        VGA90, you are expected to send in the registration form and fee
        for the VGAFontMax package.  Thank you for your support.




        อออออออ INSTRUCTIONS อออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออ


        VGA90 is a tiny utility that lets you switch your standard IBM
        VGA display (and most third-party VGA and SuperVGA boards) into
        an undocumented 90-column mode.  On some multisync monitors you
        may have to re-center the image after switching into or out of
        the 90-column mode.  The standard IBM VGA monitor has trouble
        displaying the leftmost column of the screen, although the rest
        of the screen is displayed normally.

        To see how to use VGA90, just type "VGA90 /?" at the DOS command
        prompt.  It will print out a short description of how to use
        VGA90.

        To switch into 90-column mode, type "VGA90 /ON" or just "VGA90"
        at the DOS command prompt.  To switch back, type "VGA90 /OFF".
        The "on" and "off" commands may be shortened to "VGA90 O" and
        "VGA90 F", if desired.

        VGA90 works by temporarily reprogramming the VGA board to
        display 8-bit wide characters instead of the normal 9-bit wide
        characters.  Please note that many older applications (and
        unfortunately, many of the newer ones, too) do not support
        90-column displays.  If a program does not support the 90-column
        display, the screen will appear to be sliced up and shifted
        around slightly.

        Here is a list of some programs that are known to support
        90-column (and higher) modes:

                QEdit (from SemWare)
                List (by Vernon Buerg)

        Here are some that do NOT support 90-column (or higher) modes:

                ProComm Plus (from DataStorm)
                Norton Utilities (from Peter Norton Computing)
                Microsoft Word
                SpinRite II (from Gibson Research)
                XTree Pro Gold (from Executive Systems)




